A||||23||REDHOTT88||Changing dogbone,hard job?||||5||
Z||000000||REDHOTT88||11-27-2001||07:24 PM||||I saw the "ultimate dogbone" at the fiero store. Is it a big job to change it out, mine is fine, but the red looked good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> <P>Can you just unbolt the old or does the engine fall out and it has to be retorqued, rejuvenated ...yada yada yada..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Ha Ha!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR>1988 Formula 2.8L <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (red & shiny all over!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||11-27-2001||07:28 PM||||replacing the dogbone on a scale of 1 to 10 is about a -3.  simply remove and reistall.  Takes a total on less than 5 min<P>Hank||||reg||1
Z||000002||REDHOTT88||11-27-2001||07:42 PM||||No Sh**!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> <P>Thanks a million!<P>I guess I'll be getting one...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||11-27-2001||07:44 PM||||Actually, I had to hold my engine up with a floor jack. The engine would tilt back, and I don't think you could pull it back with your arm while you put the bolts in. But yeah, it was easy. BTW, this was on a 4cyl. Not sure about the 6's.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">               <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||REDHOTT88||11-27-2001||07:45 PM||||Thanks...||||reg||10
Z||000005||Nashco||11-27-2001||08:07 PM||||If it's hard to get to "reach" to get the bolts in, there's an easy way to get past that.  Just leave the car in gear or in park, depending on if you have a manual or auto, repectively (hopefully you work on your car this way anyway).  Now rock the car back and forth, which should jar the motor back and forth along with it.  You can hold the bolt in the hole and push on it as you yank the car back and should be able to get it.<P>This is rarely necessary, I've never changed one that I could use some brute strength pulling or a cleverly placed prybar...nevertheless it's an option.<P>Like was said, -3, IMO.  Good luck!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||11-27-2001||09:25 PM||||Just make sure your engine/tranny mounts are not worn or the engine will shift. You should be able to manhandle it back into position if this happens. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||11-27-2001||09:27 PM||||You can just see mine here. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Camer008_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
