Z||000011||rogergarrison||11-27-2001||11:08 PM||||Your fav fiero friend Lisa did her own too in a few minutes, but broke a nail. LOL. put a big block of wood on floor jack so you dont dent the oil pan.||||reg||1
Z||000012||clayatandalusia||11-28-2001||12:18 AM||||you will laugh at how i did mine ...  blocked the tires (parking break actually worked it would have been easier) then ties a comealong from a beam in the shop to the engine lift mount and tightnend the come al long untill the engine got in that perfect place<P>Clay Brown <P>85' Med Royal Blue 2M4 SE 5 Speed<BR>84' White 2M4 4 Speed<BR>87' Red 2M6 5 Speed<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||revin||11-28-2001||08:16 AM||||What? I didn't have to go through all that stuff! just unbolt then put the engine part on and stick a long pipe for leverage and muscle(pull) it to the other side and put the bolt in! OR put a chain around something solid that will hold the engine where it is and take out and put in all in two steps.||||reg||1
Z||000014||avengador1||11-28-2001||02:45 PM||||Thanks for the comments on my engine bay. I cleaned out the whole mess when I had the engine out to rebuild it. I try to keep it clean because I enter it in shows. I also drive it if the weather is good and have 159000 miles on the body. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Howard_Sacks||11-28-2001||04:17 PM||||I don't think you'll run into this problem RedHott, but I haven't read about anyone else having this problem so I'll share my experience.<P>If you swap from worn rubber motor mounts to WCF's poly ones, you need a bigger dog bone because the engine will NOT move a millimeter.<P>Also, if I were you, I'd buy West Coast's dogbone.  It looks nicer.  <A HREF="http://www.howard.saturnet.net/fiero/rollercoaster/dogbones.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.howard.saturnet.net/fiero/rollercoaster/dogbones.jpg</A> <P><BR>Howard||||reg||1
Z||000016||REDHOTT88||11-28-2001||04:28 PM||||<P><BR>Yep.. That's what I want!!!<P>I don't think mine is worn, bought car from Fiero enthusiast, seems to be well cared for!<P>Is that the link their website? I clicked on it, saw the picture, but that was all..<BR><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR>1988 Formula 2.8L V6 <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (red & shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Howard_Sacks||11-28-2001||04:46 PM||||West Coast Fiero's address is:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/</A> <P>They list it under "Mounts"||||reg||1
Z||000018||tgowens||11-28-2001||06:25 PM||||The easiest way to install the dog bone is to bolt it into the rear (on the trunk side) bracket first and then use a long pry bar and pull the engine back while sliding the front bolt in -- takes less than a minute that way, and you don't need to use a floor jack.....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||REDHOTT88||11-28-2001||06:49 PM||||Thanks everyone for the help.. I appreciate it!! Looks like I'll have something to do next weekend. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L V6</B> <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red & shiny all over - with all the options!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fie Ro||11-29-2001||06:02 AM||||I just started the engine, put  it in gear, hit the breaks, let the clutch come up (slipping), engine bend to the rear and had someone put the bolt in. Voila! ||||reg||12
Z||000021||Last Dodo||12-04-2001||06:10 PM||||I got the prototype units for my GT from 8shark.com.  They are plastic but work with the original dogbone not sure about ultimate dogbone.  The engine doesn't budge even from idle to full throttle jumps.<P>I forget how much I paid but they are less expensive than a complete dogbone replacement.  I just polished the dogbone and reinstalled it.  <P>Job was easy.  I still have the original engine mounts and they had some slop in them.  I put in the back bolt made sure the tranny was in park and rocked the car back and forth until the other bolt went in.<P>Good luck on your project.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Kasdan||12-04-2001||06:40 PM||||are all you people insane? the dogbone is the hardest part!  much harder than installing, say, a flux capacitor?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  hope u know im j/k, its very easy.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000023||K2m4||12-04-2001||07:01 PM||||Lets just say this ...It takes longer to find a razorblade and cut the tape off the box than it does to install the damn thing. Or better yet it takes londer to search for the wrenches to take the bolts off the oem dig bone than it does the install the new one<P>Kerm <BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
